1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,089
Friday, the eighteenth of
January, two thousand eight.

2
00:00:04,109 --> 00:00:07,600
La Défense, the business
district just outside Paris.

3
00:00:08,589 --> 00:00:13,290
Inside one of France's biggest banks, the
people who check the traders have found

4
00:00:13,310 --> 00:00:15,019
something they cannot explain.

5
00:00:16,032 --> 00:00:21,101
A trade on one desk is supposed to be
backed by a large bank on the other side.

6
00:00:21,932 --> 00:00:24,161
There is even an e-mail confirming it.

7
00:00:25,071 --> 00:00:27,241
The e-mail does not look right.

8
00:00:28,192 --> 00:00:33,201
On Saturday, the large bank in question
says it has never heard of these trades.

9
00:00:34,252 --> 00:00:38,821
That same day, the trader who entered
them admits that he made them up.

10
00:00:39,655 --> 00:00:44,365
On Sunday afternoon, the bank finally
adds up what he has been hiding.

11
00:00:45,256 --> 00:00:50,305
It is a bet on the stock market
worth about fifty billion euros.

12
00:00:51,444 --> 00:00:54,954
That is more than the whole bank
is worth on the stock exchange.

13
00:00:56,081 --> 00:00:58,331
The trader is thirty-one years old.

14
00:00:58,861 --> 00:01:00,971
His name is Jérôme Kerviel.

15
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:07,291
His pay, salary and bonus together, is
under a hundred thousand euros a year.

16
00:01:08,427 --> 00:01:13,257
Getting rid of his bet will cost the
bank four point nine billion euros.

17
00:01:14,456 --> 00:01:19,746
Two years later, a court in Paris
orders him to pay all of it back.

18
00:01:20,876 --> 00:01:24,606
French reporters work out how long
that would take on his salary.

19
00:01:25,436 --> 00:01:30,526
One hundred seventy-seven thousand,
five hundred and thirty-six years.

20
00:01:31,677 --> 00:01:36,487
And six years after that, another
court cuts the bill to one million.

21
00:01:37,707 --> 00:01:42,147
This is how a debt of almost five billion
euros shrank by ninety-nine point nine

22
00:01:42,167 --> 00:01:44,677
eight percent.

23
00:01:45,427 --> 00:01:50,758
And how a French court came to decide that
the bank had helped lose its own money.

24
00:01:52,528 --> 00:01:56,039
He grew up in Pont-l'Abbé,
a small town in Brittany.

25
00:01:56,808 --> 00:02:00,118
His father was a blacksmith,
his mother a hairdresser.

26
00:02:01,021 --> 00:02:04,611
In two thousand, with a master's
degree in finance from Lyon,

27
00:02:05,081 --> 00:02:07,521
he joined Société Générale.

28
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:09,171
Not on a trading desk.

29
00:02:09,521 --> 00:02:13,531
In the middle office, the department
that checks the traders' work.

30
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:16,510
He spent five years there.

31
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:21,599
The bank would later write that this gave
him a very good understanding of all of

32
00:02:21,620 --> 00:02:23,909
its processing and control procedures.

33
00:02:25,155 --> 00:02:28,004
In two thousand five, he
moved to the other side.

34
00:02:28,735 --> 00:02:31,814
He became a junior trader
on a desk called Delta One,

35
00:02:31,835 --> 00:02:34,545
in the bank's equity derivatives business.

36
00:02:35,550 --> 00:02:38,120
His job, on paper, was dull.

37
00:02:39,050 --> 00:02:43,909
Buy one basket of financial instruments,
sell another basket that is almost the

38
00:02:43,930 --> 00:02:47,580
same, and earn the small
difference between their prices.

39
00:02:48,490 --> 00:02:51,120
Because the two baskets
cancel each other out,

40
00:02:51,469 --> 00:02:55,600
the bank is not supposed to be betting
on whether markets rise or fall.

41
00:02:56,718 --> 00:03:01,609
But the gaps are so small that the trades
have to be enormous to be worth anything.

42
00:03:02,338 --> 00:03:06,008
So nobody on that floor is
alarmed by very big numbers.

43
00:03:07,253 --> 00:03:12,113
For two thousand six, he was reportedly paid
a salary of seventy-four thousand euros

44
00:03:12,133 --> 00:03:15,933
and a bonus of sixty thousand.

45
00:03:15,953 --> 00:03:18,063
Modest, by trading-floor standards.

46
00:03:18,834 --> 00:03:22,473
People inside the bank later
described him as not a star.

47
00:03:22,493 --> 00:03:26,183
The governor of the Banque
de France, Christian Noyer,

48
00:03:26,554 --> 00:03:28,683
called him a computer genius.

49
00:03:29,723 --> 00:03:33,234
What he did have was something
most traders do not.

50
00:03:34,103 --> 00:03:37,274
He knew exactly how the
people checking him worked,

51
00:03:37,603 --> 00:03:39,734
because he had been one of them.

52
00:03:41,387 --> 00:03:44,516
According to the bank, the
trick was simple to describe.

53
00:03:45,167 --> 00:03:47,157
The first basket was real.

54
00:03:47,687 --> 00:03:48,977
He really bought it.

55
00:03:49,567 --> 00:03:52,977
The second basket, the one that
was supposed to cancel it out,

56
00:03:53,146 --> 00:03:54,676
was partly fiction.

57
00:03:56,008 --> 00:04:00,638
On the bank's screens, his position
looked balanced and almost risk free.

58
00:04:01,468 --> 00:04:06,818
In reality, he was making a straight bet
on where European stock markets would go.

59
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:11,560
The bank's account of how he kept
it hidden reads like a manual.

60
00:04:12,370 --> 00:04:17,319
He chose fake trades that moved no cash
and needed no immediate confirmation.

61
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:22,199
He used other employees'
computer codes to cancel trades.

62
00:04:22,810 --> 00:04:24,399
He falsified documents.

63
00:04:24,730 --> 00:04:29,689
And whenever a fake trade drew a question,
he cancelled it and booked a new one

64
00:04:29,709 --> 00:04:31,099
in a different instrument.

65
00:04:32,124 --> 00:04:36,233
Bank officials said he closed fake
trades within two or three days,

66
00:04:36,344 --> 00:04:40,904
just before the timed checks would flag
them, and then rolled the position into

67
00:04:40,924 --> 00:04:41,754
new ones.

68
00:04:42,624 --> 00:04:47,173
The chairman, Daniel Bouton,
compared it to a mutating virus.

69
00:04:47,903 --> 00:04:52,504
Outside experts doubted that anyone could
juggle sums that large on a three-day

70
00:04:52,524 --> 00:04:54,793
cycle without somebody noticing.

71
00:04:55,754 --> 00:04:58,084
He has never denied exceeding his limits.

72
00:04:58,774 --> 00:05:01,124
He has always denied
that he hid them alone.

73
00:05:02,074 --> 00:05:06,054
He told investigators that his
superiors tolerated what he was doing,

74
00:05:06,074 --> 00:05:07,884
as long as it made money.

75
00:05:08,935 --> 00:05:11,625
In two thousand seven, the bets grew.

76
00:05:12,555 --> 00:05:16,156
Investigators found positions
of around thirty billion euros,

77
00:05:16,175 --> 00:05:20,425
against a reported limit of one
hundred twenty-five million.

78
00:05:21,514 --> 00:05:25,764
On a chart drawn to scale,
the limit is barely a line.

79
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:30,650
By the summer, the bets were
down two point two billion.

80
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:35,590
By New Year's Eve, they were
up one point four billion.

81
00:05:36,620 --> 00:05:42,070
By his own account, he hid that profit too,
and declared about sixty million instead.

82
00:05:42,901 --> 00:05:46,690
He reportedly hoped for a bonus
of six hundred thousand euros.

83
00:05:47,831 --> 00:05:53,951
The bank's own internal inquiry later counted
seventy-four alerts about his activity

84
00:05:53,971 --> 00:05:56,540
that managers failed to follow up.

85
00:05:57,437 --> 00:06:01,926
Then, in January two thousand
eight, he turned the bet around.

86
00:06:02,797 --> 00:06:06,177
He was now wagering that
European markets would go up.

87
00:06:06,197 --> 00:06:11,877
Thirty billion on the Euro Stoxx,
eighteen billion on Germany's DAX,

88
00:06:11,896 --> 00:06:14,127
two billion on London.

89
00:06:15,817 --> 00:06:17,507
Markets were about to do the opposite.

90
00:06:19,456 --> 00:06:24,576
On that Sunday, the chairman, Daniel
Bouton, tells the governor of the Banque de

91
00:06:24,596 --> 00:06:25,396
France.

92
00:06:25,915 --> 00:06:27,185
Then he makes a decision.

93
00:06:27,936 --> 00:06:32,565
Close the position as fast as possible,
and say nothing until it is done.

94
00:06:33,567 --> 00:06:38,067
At a board meeting that evening, the
directors are told only that problems have

95
00:06:38,086 --> 00:06:43,367
been found in certain market activities, and
that the year's results cannot be published

96
00:06:43,387 --> 00:06:44,187
yet.

97
00:06:44,843 --> 00:06:50,333
Overnight, before Europe even
opens, Asian markets fall hard.

98
00:06:51,064 --> 00:06:55,721
Hong Kong's Hang Seng drops
around five point four percent.

99
00:06:56,122 --> 00:07:01,202
On Monday morning, a single trader at the
bank starts selling Jérôme Kerviel's position

100
00:07:01,222 --> 00:07:02,932
into a falling market.

101
00:07:04,002 --> 00:07:07,492
European stock markets lose
around six percent that day.

102
00:07:08,281 --> 00:07:12,451
The bank says it kept its selling to under
a tenth of the trading in each market,

103
00:07:12,541 --> 00:07:15,011
so as not to move prices on its own.

104
00:07:15,822 --> 00:07:22,084
On the first day, its share of Euro Stoxx
futures trading was eight point one percent.

105
00:07:22,485 --> 00:07:25,435
Wall Street is closed that
Monday for a public holiday.

106
00:07:26,125 --> 00:07:30,235
On Tuesday, the American central
bank does something it rarely does.

107
00:07:30,685 --> 00:07:35,514
It cuts interest rates in an emergency
move, before its scheduled meeting.

108
00:07:36,410 --> 00:07:38,500
Three quarters of a percentage point.

109
00:07:39,270 --> 00:07:44,190
A Federal Reserve spokesperson later said
the central bank had not known about the

110
00:07:44,210 --> 00:07:46,560
French bank's situation when it decided.

111
00:07:47,779 --> 00:07:50,589
By Wednesday evening,
the position is gone.

112
00:07:51,539 --> 00:07:52,409
So is the money.

113
00:07:53,159 --> 00:07:57,869
The bank puts the final loss at
four point nine billion euros.

114
00:07:59,011 --> 00:08:01,381
And that figure is already a net number.

115
00:08:02,211 --> 00:08:06,821
According to the investigation, selling his
bets lost about six point three billion.

116
00:08:07,631 --> 00:08:12,311
The bank subtracted the one point four
billion he had made in two thousand seven,

117
00:08:12,331 --> 00:08:14,401
which it had already counted as profit.

118
00:08:14,871 --> 00:08:19,380
So the gain he had hidden ended up
making the headline loss smaller.

119
00:08:20,274 --> 00:08:23,463
The chairman's defence was that
waiting would have been worse.

120
00:08:24,254 --> 00:08:29,374
If a war had broken out on Monday, he said,
or if markets had fallen thirty percent,

121
00:08:29,394 --> 00:08:31,783
the bank risked the worst.

122
00:08:32,374 --> 00:08:35,244
Had the market learned of the
position first, he argued,

123
00:08:36,014 --> 00:08:40,103
traders could have bet against it, and the
loss could have been ten times bigger.

124
00:08:41,118 --> 00:08:44,988
On Thursday, before the markets
open, the bank tells the world.

125
00:08:45,598 --> 00:08:49,858
In the same announcement it reports heavy
losses on American mortgage investments,

126
00:08:49,878 --> 00:08:53,108
and it will soon need
billions in new capital.

127
00:08:54,114 --> 00:08:58,864
The chairman calls the trader a
crook, a fraudster and a terrorist.

128
00:09:00,004 --> 00:09:02,594
Newspapers speculate that
the trader has fled.

129
00:09:03,264 --> 00:09:04,063
He has not.

130
00:09:04,923 --> 00:09:08,333
On Saturday the twenty-sixth, he
presents himself to the police.

131
00:09:09,331 --> 00:09:12,581
Two days later, he is charged
with breach of trust,

132
00:09:12,850 --> 00:09:16,640
forgery, and illegally entering
data into the bank's computers.

133
00:09:17,610 --> 00:09:22,660
The investigating judges refuse the
prosecutor's request to add attempted fraud.

134
00:09:23,171 --> 00:09:26,081
And there is one thing
he is not accused of.

135
00:09:26,431 --> 00:09:29,400
Putting a single euro in his own pocket.

136
00:09:30,453 --> 00:09:32,823
The judges let him go
home under supervision.

137
00:09:33,613 --> 00:09:37,363
The prosecutors appeal, arguing
he might contact accomplices,

138
00:09:37,853 --> 00:09:41,383
which is odd, because the
bank insists he acted alone.

139
00:09:42,453 --> 00:09:46,573
On the eve of the hearing, a broker
he had texted is taken into custody,

140
00:09:46,593 --> 00:09:48,943
and the messages leak to the press.

141
00:09:49,493 --> 00:09:52,743
The appeal court sends
Kerviel to La Santé prison.

142
00:09:53,373 --> 00:09:56,073
The broker is released and never charged.

143
00:09:56,093 --> 00:10:01,543
Kerviel spends about six weeks behind
bars before he is let out again.

144
00:10:03,341 --> 00:10:05,691
The regulators look at the bank too.

145
00:10:06,701 --> 00:10:12,461
In July two thousand eight, France's banking
commission fines Société Générale four

146
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:18,341
million euros, citing serious failures in
its internal controls that lasted through

147
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:20,812
the whole of two thousand seven.

148
00:10:21,212 --> 00:10:25,962
The chairman and the head of investment
banking both eventually leave their posts.

149
00:10:26,772 --> 00:10:27,882
The bank survives.

150
00:10:29,006 --> 00:10:33,606
His trial opens in Paris
in June two thousand ten.

151
00:10:33,626 --> 00:10:37,826
On the fifth of October, the
presiding judge, Dominique Pauthe,

152
00:10:37,846 --> 00:10:40,176
reads the verdict to a packed room.

153
00:10:41,120 --> 00:10:43,630
Through his deliberate
actions, the court says,

154
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:49,450
he endangered the solvency of a bank that
employed one hundred forty thousand people,

155
00:10:49,780 --> 00:10:51,010
including himself.

156
00:10:51,941 --> 00:10:56,041
It finds that none of his bosses would
have allowed bets bigger than the bank's

157
00:10:56,061 --> 00:10:57,050
own capital.

158
00:10:57,540 --> 00:11:02,740
His actions, the ruling says, without a
doubt threatened the public order of the

159
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:03,730
world economy.

160
00:11:04,890 --> 00:11:08,420
Five years in prison,
two of them suspended.

161
00:11:09,130 --> 00:11:12,050
And damages equal to
the bank's entire loss.

162
00:11:12,070 --> 00:11:15,600
Four point nine billion euros.

163
00:11:16,530 --> 00:11:20,040
He is also banned from
working in finance for life.

164
00:11:20,859 --> 00:11:24,179
By then he was working as
a computer consultant,

165
00:11:24,199 --> 00:11:28,168
for about two thousand three
hundred euros a month.

166
00:11:29,303 --> 00:11:32,873
His lawyer says his client is
paying for an entire system.

167
00:11:33,663 --> 00:11:38,093
Then he tells the reporters outside that
he hopes they will all donate a euro.

168
00:11:39,235 --> 00:11:42,345
The bank's spokeswoman
calls the damages symbolic.

169
00:11:43,335 --> 00:11:45,284
Nobody expects the money to be paid.

170
00:11:46,240 --> 00:11:51,620
The obvious comparison was Nick Leeson,
whose unauthorised trades in Singapore lost

171
00:11:51,639 --> 00:11:57,300
more than a billion dollars and sank
Barings Bank in nineteen ninety-five.

172
00:11:57,320 --> 00:11:59,429
Leeson served three and a half years.

173
00:12:00,180 --> 00:12:04,920
His agent let it be known that Leeson
would give one exclusive interview on the

174
00:12:04,940 --> 00:12:05,840
Kerviel verdict.

175
00:12:05,860 --> 00:12:07,210
For a fee.

176
00:12:08,058 --> 00:12:13,008
In October two thousand twelve, the Paris
court of appeal confirms everything.

177
00:12:13,558 --> 00:12:14,668
The prison term.

178
00:12:14,899 --> 00:12:16,488
The four point nine billion.

179
00:12:17,525 --> 00:12:22,826
Then, in March two thousand fourteen,
France's highest court splits the case in

180
00:12:22,846 --> 00:12:23,645
two.

181
00:12:23,985 --> 00:12:25,335
The conviction stands.

182
00:12:25,625 --> 00:12:27,075
The damages do not.

183
00:12:27,685 --> 00:12:31,015
The bank, the judges find,
made its own mistakes,

184
00:12:31,365 --> 00:12:35,455
and those mistakes helped the fraud
grow and helped cause the loss.

185
00:12:36,085 --> 00:12:39,015
So he cannot simply be
made to pay for all of it.

186
00:12:39,948 --> 00:12:43,778
A month earlier, he had spoken
briefly with Pope Francis in Rome.

187
00:12:44,688 --> 00:12:49,769
Afterwards he set off back to France on
foot, as a protest against what he called

188
00:12:49,788 --> 00:12:51,698
the tyranny of the markets.

189
00:12:52,804 --> 00:12:57,034
In May, at the Italian border, he
is arrested to serve his sentence.

190
00:12:58,184 --> 00:13:01,314
He is held at Fleury-Mérogis,
south of Paris.

191
00:13:02,064 --> 00:13:04,924
On the eighth of September
two thousand fourteen,

192
00:13:04,944 --> 00:13:08,314
after a total of one hundred
fifty days behind bars,

193
00:13:08,824 --> 00:13:10,874
he leaves with an electronic tag.

194
00:13:12,681 --> 00:13:18,171
In January two thousand sixteen, the case
cracks open from an unexpected side.

195
00:13:19,181 --> 00:13:24,121
French media publish a secretly recorded
conversation with a former deputy prosecutor

196
00:13:24,141 --> 00:13:26,291
who had worked on the investigation.

197
00:13:27,221 --> 00:13:30,911
In it, she says the inquiry
was steered by the bank.

198
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:36,241
A former commander of the financial police
who had worked on the case also voices

199
00:13:36,261 --> 00:13:37,901
doubts about the bank's account.

200
00:13:37,921 --> 00:13:43,350
In June two thousand sixteen,
something strange happens.

201
00:13:43,841 --> 00:13:49,441
A Paris labour tribunal rules that the
bank fired him without genuine or serious

202
00:13:49,461 --> 00:13:54,211
cause, and orders it to pay him around
four hundred fifty thousand euros.

203
00:13:55,181 --> 00:13:58,630
The bank calls the ruling
scandalous, and appeals.

204
00:13:59,764 --> 00:14:04,793
Three months later, the appeal court in
Versailles rules on what he really owes.

205
00:14:05,563 --> 00:14:08,153
It finds him partly
responsible for the loss.

206
00:14:08,584 --> 00:14:13,104
But it says the loss could not have happened
without the bank's woefully inadequate

207
00:14:13,124 --> 00:14:13,923
oversight.

208
00:14:14,895 --> 00:14:18,325
The new figure is one million euros.

209
00:14:19,346 --> 00:14:24,606
His lawyer, David Koubbi, says the court
has wiped out ninety-nine point nine eight

210
00:14:24,626 --> 00:14:25,787
percent of the sum.

211
00:14:25,807 --> 00:14:30,256
Kerviel says he still believes
he owes the bank nothing.

212
00:14:31,321 --> 00:14:33,331
There is another number in this story.

213
00:14:33,681 --> 00:14:38,961
Because the bank recorded the loss, the
French state granted it a tax break of about

214
00:14:38,980 --> 00:14:41,291
two point two billion euros.

215
00:14:42,061 --> 00:14:47,121
The Versailles ruling did not change that,
and critics later took the tax break

216
00:14:47,141 --> 00:14:47,941
to court.

217
00:14:48,868 --> 00:14:52,498
Two years later, the courts
close the doors he had opened.

218
00:14:53,168 --> 00:14:58,248
In September two thousand eighteen, France's
court of review rejects his bid for

219
00:14:58,268 --> 00:14:59,677
a new criminal trial.

220
00:15:00,348 --> 00:15:05,468
It calls a former police investigator's
doubts about the case impressions that rest

221
00:15:05,488 --> 00:15:07,038
on nothing precise.

222
00:15:07,548 --> 00:15:11,358
In December, an appeal court
overturns the labour tribunal.

223
00:15:11,848 --> 00:15:14,148
The firing was justified after all.

224
00:15:14,168 --> 00:15:20,568
In March two thousand twenty-one, France's
highest court rejects his last appeal

225
00:15:20,588 --> 00:15:21,598
on that point.

226
00:15:22,576 --> 00:15:23,785
He has not stopped.

227
00:15:24,656 --> 00:15:29,676
In late two thousand twenty-four, a
four-part documentary series brings him back

228
00:15:29,696 --> 00:15:33,245
in front of the cameras, alongside
the bank's former chairman.

229
00:15:34,255 --> 00:15:39,456
A few weeks later, on French television,
he says he has new evidence and intends

230
00:15:39,475 --> 00:15:43,346
to ask, once again, for
his trial to be reopened.

231
00:15:44,534 --> 00:15:45,804
So here is the account.

232
00:15:46,554 --> 00:15:49,004
A hidden bet of fifty billion euros.

233
00:15:49,615 --> 00:15:51,724
A loss of four point nine billion.

234
00:15:52,334 --> 00:15:54,204
A first bill for all of it.

235
00:15:54,694 --> 00:15:56,554
A final bill of one million.

236
00:15:56,574 --> 00:15:59,864
One hundred fifty days behind bars.

237
00:16:00,475 --> 00:16:04,004
And a tax break for the bank
of two point two billion.

238
00:16:05,008 --> 00:16:10,298
His conviction for breach of trust,
forgery and computer offences is final.

239
00:16:10,968 --> 00:16:12,217
So is the one million.

240
00:16:12,828 --> 00:16:15,358
He has kept saying that
his superiors knew.

241
00:16:16,028 --> 00:16:18,138
No court has found that they did.

242
00:16:19,118 --> 00:16:23,988
But the last court to put a price on that
January did not put it on him alone.

243
00:16:24,698 --> 00:16:29,588
It put most of it on the bank that let a
junior trader bet more than it was worth,

244
00:16:29,938 --> 00:16:33,668
and did not notice until a
confirmation e-mail looked wrong.
